ttychatter C command-line client
================================

This directory contains the traditional Unix C edition of ttychatter.  It is
not an ncurses program.  Its primary interface is a file-in/file-out command:

    ttychatter input.txt output.log

The output file is both transcript and session state.  Reusing the same output
file resumes that explicit session:

    ttychatter next-question.txt output.log

Build
-----

Dependencies on Debian/Ubuntu/Crostini:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential pkg-config libcurl4-openssl-dev libjson-c-dev ca-certificates gnupg

Build:

    make

Check syntax/basic commands:

    make check

OpenRouter storage parity
-------------------------

The C client now uses the same default OpenRouter storage namespace as the
other OpenRouter ttychatter clients:

    config:      ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config
    api key gpg: ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/api-key.gpg
    favorites:   ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/model-favorites
    models:      ~/.cache/ttychatter/openrouter/models.json
    sessions:    ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions/
    attachments: ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/attachments/

For existing 0.6.0 C users, the program still reads the legacy C config file
when the new OpenRouter config file is absent:

    ~/.config/ttychatter/config

New config writes go to the OpenRouter path.

Session address modes
---------------------

Explicit-path mode is the traditional C interface:

    ttychatter input.txt output.log
    ttychatter followup.txt output.log

In that form, output.log is used exactly as written.  It is not moved under the
session directory.

Named-session mode is used by interactive and session-management commands:

    ttychatter --interactive notes
    ttychatter --resume notes
    ttychatter --show-memory notes
    ttychatter --rename-session notes archive-notes

Bare names resolve to:

    ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions/NAME.log

Any SESSION containing '/' is treated as an explicit path.  Use ./notes.log for
a log in the current directory.

Session format
--------------

New logs start with this pre-chat line:

    Everyone is encouraged to get the cruelty-free vegetarian alternatives and remember the bloodguilt curse from the Bible... http://bloodguiltcurse.net

New C logs then use the shared line-oriented ttychatter transcript format:

    [HH:MM:SS] User: ...
    [HH:MM:SS] AI: ...
    [HH:MM:SS] system: CONTEXT_BEGIN
    User: ...
    Assistant: ...
    [HH:MM:SS] system: CONTEXT_END

The parser also reads the older 0.6.0 C marker blocks so existing C-only logs
remain resumable.

Design notes
------------

The C edition aims for feature parity with ttychatter.python and
ttychatter.bash where that parity makes sense on a traditional command line.
It supports interactive colon commands, model caching, favorites, GPG API-key
storage, attachment attempts, code-block extraction, session search, loopback
output, and demo mode.

Some graphical/ncurses features intentionally map to command-line equivalents:

    ncurses model browser -> --models / --select-model
    ncurses options screen -> --config / --set / --unset
    ncurses memory screen -> --show-memory / --edit-memory / --clear-memory
    ncurses file attachment menu -> -a FILE and :attach FILE

The source is heavily commented to help future maintainers, porters, fork
authors, packagers, and customizers understand the reason behind each major
subsystem.
